  /* ---------- Hand-written set 2: harder classics with full walkthroughs ---------- */
  ["prob",3,"num","What is the expected number of fair coin flips until you see heads, tails, tails (HTT)?",8,"Track progress toward HTT: nothing, H, HT.","States: E₀ = 2 + E₁ (wait for an H), E₁ = 1 + ½E₁ + ½E₂, E₂ = 1 + ½E₁. Solving gives E₂ = 4, E₁ = 6, E₀ = 8."],
  ["prob",3,"num","A fair coin is flipped until either HHT or HTT appears. What is the probability HHT appears first?",2/3,"Once you have an H, condition on the next flip.","From H: another H guarantees HHT eventually wins (HH can only end in HHT). From HT: H restarts from H, T finishes HTT. Solving p = ½ + ½(½p) gives p = 2/3."],
  ["prob",3,"num","A fair coin is flipped until THH or HHH appears. What is the probability THH appears first?",7/8,"HHH can only win if the first three flips are all heads.","If any tail appears before HHH, the first HH after that tail is preceded by a T, so THH comes first. HHH wins only on HHH at the start: 1/8. So THH wins with 7/8."],
  ["prob",3,"num","You keep adding independent Uniform(0,1) numbers until the total exceeds 1. What is the expected number of numbers you add? (Decimal.)",Math.E,"P(the first n numbers sum to less than 1) = 1/n!.","P(N > n) = 1/n!, so E[N] = Σ P(N > n) = 1 + 1 + 1/2! + 1/3! + … = e ≈ 2.718."],
  ["prob",3,"num","b and c are independent Uniform(0,1). What is the probability that x² + bx + c = 0 has real roots?",1/12,"Real roots need b² ≥ 4c.","P(c ≤ b²/4) = ∫₀¹ b²/4 db = 1/12."],
  ["prob",3,"num","A stick of length 1 is broken at two independent uniform points. What is the expected length of the shortest piece?",1/9,"The three pieces behave like uniform spacings.","For n uniform cut points the shortest of n+1 pieces averages (1/(n+1)) × (1/(n+1)) = 1/9 when n = 2."],
  ["prob",2,"num","What is the expected number of rolls of a fair die until you get two sixes in a row?",42,"First get one six, then you need one more immediately.","E = E₁ + 1 + (5/6)E with E₁ = 6, so E/6 = 7 and E = 42."],
  ["prob",2,"num","You roll three fair dice. What is the probability their sum is exactly 10?",1/8,"Count ordered triples.","27 of the 216 ordered outcomes sum to 10, so 27/216 = 1/8."],
  ["prob",2,"num","Two points are chosen independently and uniformly on [0, 1]. What is the probability they are within ½ of each other?",3/4,"Draw the unit square and remove two corner triangles.","|X − Y| ≥ ½ is two triangles each of area ⅛, so the answer is 1 − ¼ = 3/4."],
  ["prob",2,"num","One coin is fair and one has heads on both sides. You pick one at random, flip it three times and see three heads. What is the probability you picked the two-headed coin?",8/9,"Compare the chance of three heads under each coin.","P(HHH | two-headed) = 1, P(HHH | fair) = 1/8. Posterior = 1/(1 + 1/8) = 8/9."],
  ["prob",2,"num","A family keeps having children until they have at least one boy and one girl. Boys and girls are equally likely. What is the expected number of children?",3,"After the first child, wait for the other sex.","The first child is either sex; then each child is the other sex with probability ½, a geometric wait of 2. Total 1 + 2 = 3."],
  ["prob",2,"num","X and Y are independent Uniform(0,1). What is P(X + Y < 1.5)?",7/8,"Only a small corner of the square has X + Y ≥ 1.5.","The corner X + Y ≥ 1.5 is a triangle with legs ½, area ⅛. So P = 7/8."],
  ["prob",3,"num","You turn over cards from a shuffled deck until the first red card. What is the expected number of black cards you turn over before it?",26/27,"The 26 red cards split the black cards into 27 gaps.","Each black card is equally likely to fall in any of the 27 gaps, so it comes before the first red with probability 1/27. Expected: 26/27."],
  ["prob",3,"num","A revolver has 6 chambers in a circle with bullets in 2 adjacent chambers. The cylinder is spun once and the first pull is empty. If you pull again without spinning, what is your probability of surviving?",3/4,"Given an empty chamber, which chamber comes next?","Of the 4 empty chambers, exactly 1 is followed by a bullet. Pulling again survives with probability 3/4 (spinning would give 4/6 = 2/3)."],
  ["prob",3,"num","What is the expected number of cycles in a uniformly random permutation of 10 elements? (Decimal.)",1+1/2+1/3+1/4+1/5+1/6+1/7+1/8+1/9+1/10,"Count cycles by where each new cycle starts.","E[cycles] = H₁₀ = 1 + 1/2 + … + 1/10 ≈ 2.929."],
  ["prob",3,"num","You roll a fair die repeatedly until a 6 appears. Given that every roll was even, what is the expected number of rolls?",1.5,"Conditioning on 'all even' rules out long sequences.","Stop at the first roll that is odd or a 6 (probability 2/3 per roll), so the stopping time is geometric with mean 3/2. Whether it stopped on a 6 or an odd number doesn't depend on when it stopped, so conditioning on 'it was a 6' leaves the mean at 3/2 = 1.5."],
  ["prob",2,"num","X and Y are independent N(0,1). What is the expected value of max(X, Y)? (Decimal.)",1/Math.sqrt(Math.PI),"max(X, Y) = (X + Y)/2 + |X − Y|/2.","X − Y ~ N(0, 2), so E|X − Y| = √2 · √(2/π) = 2/√π. Half of that is 1/√π ≈ 0.5642."],
  ["stats",2,"num","X and Y are independent N(0,1). What is the correlation between X and X + Y? (Decimal.)",Math.SQRT1_2,"Cov(X, X + Y) = Var(X).","Cov = 1, SD(X) = 1, SD(X + Y) = √2. Correlation 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071."],
  ["stats",2,"num","Two uncorrelated assets have variances 1 and 4. In the minimum-variance portfolio, what fraction goes into the first asset?",0.8,"With zero correlation, weight each asset by 1/variance.","Weights ∝ 1/1 and 1/4, so the first gets 1/(1 + ¼) = 0.8."],
  ["stats",2,"num","A coin flipped 100 times lands heads 60 times. If it were fair, how many standard deviations above the mean is that?",2,"SD of the head count is √(np(1 − p)).","Mean 50, SD √25 = 5. (60 − 50)/5 = 2."],
  ["options",2,"num","A stock at $100 will be $120 or $90 in one period, with zero interest rates. What is the fair price of a call with strike $100? (Decimal.)",20/3,"Find the probability that makes the stock a fair bet.","Risk-neutral q solves 120q + 90(1 − q) = 100, so q = 1/3. The call pays 20 in the up state: 20 × 1/3 ≈ 6.667."],
  ["options",2,"num","In the same one-step tree ($100 goes to $120 or $90, zero rates), how many shares of stock hedge one call with strike $100?",2/3,"Delta is the change in option value over the change in stock price.","(20 − 0)/(120 − 90) = 2/3 of a share."],
  ["options",3,"num","Using Black–Scholes, what is the price of a 1-year at-the-money straddle on a $100 stock with 20% volatility and zero rates? (To the cent.)",15.93,"An ATM call here is worth about 0.4 × S × σ × √T.","Each leg is worth about $7.97 (exactly 7.966 for the call; the put is equal at zero rates), so the straddle is about $15.93."],
  ["options",1,"mc","What is the price of a European call with strike 0 on a stock that pays no dividends?",["0","The stock price","The strike","It can't be priced"],1,"The option is certain to be exercised.","With K = 0 you always exercise and receive the stock, so the call is worth S."],
  ["brain",2,"num","There are 100 doors and one prize. You pick a door; the host, who knows where the prize is, opens 98 empty doors you didn't pick. What is your probability of winning if you switch?",99/100,"Your first pick is right only 1% of the time.","Switching wins whenever the first pick was wrong: 99/100."],
  ["brain",2,"num","Nine balls look identical, but one is heavier. With a balance scale, what is the minimum number of weighings that always finds it?",2,"Each weighing has three outcomes.","Weigh 3 against 3: the heavy ball is in the heavier group or the 3 left out. Then weigh 1 against 1 from that group. Two weighings, since 3² = 9."],
  ["brain",3,"num","Four people cross a bridge at night with one torch. At most two cross at once, a pair moves at the slower person's speed, and the torch must be carried. Their times are 1, 2, 5 and 10 minutes. What is the shortest total time?",17,"Send the two slowest across together.","1 and 2 cross (2), 1 returns (1), 5 and 10 cross (10), 2 returns (2), 1 and 2 cross (2): 17 minutes."],
  ["brain",2,"num","What is 1/(1·2) + 1/(2·3) + … + 1/(99·100)? (Decimal.)",0.99,"Write 1/(k(k+1)) = 1/k − 1/(k+1).","The sum telescopes to 1 − 1/100 = 0.99."],
  ["brain",2,"num","In how many ways can a 2 × 10 board be tiled with 1 × 2 dominoes?",89,"Look at how the left edge is covered.","Either one vertical domino (leaving 2 × 9) or two horizontal ones (leaving 2 × 8): f(n) = f(n − 1) + f(n − 2), the Fibonacci numbers, so f(10) = 89."],
  ["stats",1,"num","An even-money bet wins 55% of the time. What fraction of your bankroll does the Kelly criterion say to bet?",0.1,"For even money, Kelly = 2p − 1.","f* = p − q = 0.55 − 0.45 = 0.10, so bet 10% of your bankroll each time."],
  ["stats",2,"num","A bet pays 2 to 1 (win $2 per $1 staked) and wins 40% of the time. What fraction of your bankroll does Kelly say to bet?",0.1,"Kelly: f* = (bp − q)/b with b the net odds.","f* = (2 × 0.4 − 0.6)/2 = 0.2/2 = 0.10."],
  ["stats",2,"num","A bet pays 2 to 1 and wins 60% of the time. What is the Kelly fraction?",0.4,"Kelly: f* = (bp − q)/b.","f* = (2 × 0.6 − 0.4)/2 = 0.8/2 = 0.40. A big edge, but most traders would still bet a fraction of this."],
  ["code",2,"mc","Building a binary heap from n unsorted items with the standard bottom-up method takes…",["O(log n)","O(n)","O(n log n)","O(n²)"],1,"Most nodes are near the bottom and sift down only a little.","The total sift-down work is a convergent series times n, so heapify is O(n)."],
  ["code",1,"mc","Finding two numbers in an array that sum to a target, using a hash set of values seen so far, takes…",["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n²)"],2,"One pass, constant-time lookups.","For each number, check whether target − x was seen: O(n) time on average."]
